- models/ — trained model artifacts (logistic_churn.pkl, tree_churn.pkl present)
- docs/ — final documentation and screenshots placeholders
- tests/test_customer_cleaner.py — small unit test for cleaner

3. Phase 1 — Data engineering & ingestion
----------------------------------------
Objective
- Produce a canonical cleaned data table (staging) suitable for feature engineering and downstream analytics.

Why we did it
- The raw Telco CSV contains inconsistent types for numeric fields and some empty/blank values that must be normalized before aggregation, modeling, and DB ingestion.
- A reusable cleaner centralizes the rules (coercion, imputation, derived fields) so notebooks and scripts produce consistent datasets.

What I implemented
- scripts/customer_cleaner.py — new module implementing CustomerCleaner with the following responsibilities:
  - Load CSV and standardize column names
  - Coerce MonthlyCharges and TotalCharges to numeric (float)
  - Impute missing TotalCharges: for tenure == 0 set to MonthlyCharges (or 0), otherwise approximate by MonthlyCharges * tenure for missing values
  - Derive churn_flag (1/0), per-service flags (OnlineSecurity_flag etc.), service_count (sum of flags), tenure_bucket categorical feature
  - Export cleaned CSV (to_csv) and load into DB (to_sql), with default staging table name `main1`

Key file mapping & code locations
- Cleaner: scripts/customer_cleaner.py
- Ingest script (modified): load.py (now uses CustomerCleaner and writes to `main1`)

How to run (commands)
- Clean the raw CSV and write CSV:
  python scripts/customer_cleaner.py --input "WA_Fn-UseC_-Telco-Customer-Churn(in).csv" --output data/cleaned_customer.csv

- Load cleaned data into the staging table `main1`:
  python load.py

Output and evidence
- data/cleaned_customer.csv — cleaned artifact (generate locally)
- Database table `main1` — staging table with numeric MonthlyCharges and TotalCharges
- Notebook evidence: DataEngineering.ipynb shows head and data summary

Screenshot placeholder (insert after running the commands)
- docs/screenshots/phase1_cleaned_head.png — first 10 rows of the cleaned CSV or notebook cell

Questions & caveats
- Changing ORM types (to Float) does not alter your local DB; if existing tables were created with text columns, you should re-import or run an ALTER TABLE to convert types before performing numeric aggregates.
- load.py was updated to write to `main1` (lowercase) to match the ORM. If you previously wrote to `Main1`, please drop or rename the old table.

4. Phase 2 — Exploration & feature engineering
----------------------------------------------
Objective
- Explore data distributions, missingness, and create derived features required by the model and business logic.

Why we did it
- EDA reveals variable distributions and relationships with churn, and feature engineering encodes domain knowledge (e.g., tenure buckets, streaming bundle flag) that improves model signal.

What was implemented (or exists)
- Notebooks: DataAnalyzing.ipynb (EDA) and FeatureEngineering.ipynb (feature construction) — kept in the repo and produce plots and aggregated summaries.
- scripts/Load_Features.py modified to ensure TotalCharges numeric and to write features to `customer_features` table.

Derived features created (examples)
- churn_flag — binary target derived from Churn
- service_count — count of positive binary service flags (PhoneService, OnlineSecurity, etc.)
- tenure_bucket — categorical binning of tenure
- online_service flags — boolean flags converted to 0/1 to simplify modeling

How to run
- Open and step through FeatureEngineering.ipynb to regenerate customer_features.csv, or run the Load_Features.py which expects an existing customer_features.csv or pre-cleaned input.
  python Load_Features.py

Outputs and evidence
- customer_features.csv — derived features
- DB table: customer_features
- Notebook outputs: histograms, churn rates per bucket, correlation tables

Screenshot placeholders
- docs/screenshots/phase2_feature_preview.png — snippet of customer_features.csv showing derived columns
- docs/screenshots/phase2_eda_plot.png — sample EDA plot (distribution or churn by bucket)

Implementation notes
- FeatureEngineering.ipynb contains the logic used to compute feature_count and flags. The cleaner produces consistent input types so the feature script can run deterministically.

5. Phase 3 — Modeling, evaluation & model usage
-----------------------------------------------
Objective
- Train and evaluate one or more models for churn prediction and provide a way to score new records via API.

Why we did it
- To provide a reproducible modeling pipeline and a deployable scoring interface that downstream systems or a frontend can call.

Modeling artifacts and location
- ML notebooks: ML_prep.ipynb (data prep and training) and UsingModel.ipynb (example usage)
- models/: contains trained artifacts (examples in repo: logistic_churn.pkl, tree_churn.pkl). These are pickled scikit-learn estimators saved from notebooks.
- routers.py: exposes POST /customer/predict_churn and other endpoints for summary and high-risk customer listing.

Model training summary (recommended steps)
- Use cleaned features from Phase 2
- Split into train/test (e.g., 80/20), use stratification on churn_flag due to class imbalance
- Evaluate using metrics appropriate to business: AUC-ROC, Precision-Recall, confusion matrix, and a calibration check
- Persist model artifact with joblib (preferred) or pickle when acceptable

Model card (brief)
- Model type: scikit-learn estimator (check models/ for specific type)
- Input schema expected by API (ChurnPredictionRequest):
  - tenure (int)
  - monthly_charges (float)
  - contract (str) — API converts contract string into a contract_flag (month-to-month -> 1 else 0)
  - service_count (int)
- Output: JSON containing risk_score (probability), prediction (Likely to churn / Unlikely to churn)
- Performance: See ML_prep.ipynb for measured metrics. (Include screenshots of the evaluation section in docs/screenshots/phase3_model_metrics.png)

Model usage via API
- Start API: uvicorn main:app --reload
- Secure header: x-api-key: Aniruddh (verify_api_key is a simple header check in routers.py)
- Example scoring request (curl):
  curl -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:8000/customer/predict_churn" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" -H "x-api-key: Aniruddh" \
    -d '{"tenure":12,"monthly_charges":70.0,"contract":"Month-to-month","service_count":3}'

How the API loads models
- The routers.py model loader scans models/ for common file types (.joblib, .pkl, .pickle). It attempts joblib.load first, and then pickle.load as fallback.
- If a loaded model provides predict_proba, the API uses the positive-class probability as the risk_score; otherwise it uses predict.
- If no model loads successfully, the endpoint returns a documented stub response. This design allows keeping model artifacts in models/ without changing environment variables.

Screenshot placeholders
- docs/screenshots/phase3_model_metrics.png — evaluation outputs (ROC, confusion matrix)
- docs/screenshots/phase3_predict_response.png — API JSON example

Notes and recommendations
- For production readiness, consider:
  - Adding versioning to model artifacts (e.g., models/{model-name}-v1.joblib)
  - A small model card (models/MODEL_CARD.md) describing feature order and preprocessing required
  - Use joblib over pickle for scikit-learn models and track dependencies (scikit-learn version)

